Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:00.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  after germany's most significant general  election in years  the country's vice chancellor olef  scholz is tasked with brokering a  coalition deal  his centre-left social democrat party  narrowly took first place in the vote  but not by enough to put them in power  two much smaller parties the greens and  the liberal fdp  will be key to the three-way coalition  needed to form a government  the economist's editor-in-chief zanny  minton bedos was joined by berlin bureau  chief tom nuttall and jurg lao did sites  foreign policy coordinator to discuss  what the results of the election mean  for the future of germany  the departure of chancellor merkel in  itself is a pretty momentous event for  germany and indeed for europe but the  results of this election will change a  lot or will they what will they mean for  germany what will they mean for europe  and what are the wider implications for  the rest of the world the most exciting  new development is is the greens and the  ftp  uh being king makers so that's a really  new development that we haven't seen  before because they they come from  different milieus they the people who  vote for them don't especially like each  other  so um there is a lot to be overcome  there the two traditional people's  parties the union and the spd they got  around about half of the vote and that  number has been going down and down and  down and down over the years as it has  done in other countries but i think  what's different in germany is that that  fragmentation has not come at the  expense of the political center  considered its broadest sense so we've  already spoken about how the fdp and  particularly the green party did well  the share of the seats that the  extremist parties if you want to call  them that the afd on the right and  de-linker on the left the share of seats  that they will have in the bundestag has  shrunk so what you see is a  strengthening of the political center in  germany despite the fragmentation and  that i think is a distinct german story  that you don't necessarily find in other  european countries that have experienced  political fragmentation  in the end these negotiations are about  characters and who's going to lead so um  maybe jurg let's start with you because  olaf schultz who is uh the chancellor  candidate of the spd and thus  currently the man tipped to be the most  likely next chancellor he was the mayor  of hamburg where you are give us a quick  pen portrait you know him what's he like  he's a very calm rational guy  he likes to present himself as the safe  pair of hands and  in the social democratic spectrum he's  someone who  was a reformist with a gerhard schroeder  very much in favor of the reforms of the  welfare state  that were not popular with social  democrats and he's come a long way in in  um yeah um embracing  a little bit more of a left-wing agenda  but still  um presenting it uh in a very rational  calm way so it's not to scare off any  uh any voters so i'd like to turn now to  the international implications of this  vote in the post-merkel era which is the  one we're going to now  what will change in terms of germany's  role in the world well one factor is  that the next chancellor whoever it will  be  will be  a somewhat weaker figure than mrs merkel  from everything we just pointed out uh  the more fragmented political landscape  the  uh  the three-way coalition  um  so  this might mean that um  the bargaining power of germany on the  european stage might also  be somewhat diminished if we get a green  foreign minister  and  a social democratic chancellor we might  have a very interesting tension the  greens have somewhat tougher positions  on russia and china they were very very  critical about north stream too the  pipeline  uh connecting germany to to russia's gas  um  and olaf schultz  and also army russia were very very  supportive of this project so  it's it's going to be very interesting  to see how this plays out the  interesting thing about german coalition  agreements is that they're incredibly  detailed and the parties lay out what  their view is on all kinds of policy  matters and they lay out you know  specific tax policies spending policies  all manner of things but the problem  with foreign policy is that events  happen you know the americans leave  afghanistan at short notice you know  something happens and you can't  presumably write a coalition agreement  that foresees every possible thing that  could happen in the next few years where  is germany going to end up in your view  vis-a-vis russia and then vis-a-vis  china  russia i think is really a kind of a  problem to be managed something to be  contained um angela merkel held together  the eu which imposed sanctions on russia  after its um annexation of crimea the  invasion of eastern ukraine um a few  years ago and those sanctions have held  ever since i see no reason for the next  chancellor whoever it is  to change to change course on that the  chinese question i think is much more  complicated and probably in the long run  much more important um angela merkel of  course throughout her time as chancellor  um had an engagement first policy um  with china i think she was fascinated by  its rise she's sometimes accused of just  being a mechanicalist you know  subjecting german foreign policy to its  um trading relationship with china  certainly german exporters did very well  out of the rise of china but i think  there was more to the merkel line than  mercantilism i think what she wanted to  do um and it was a reasonable uh is a  reasonable thing to want um was to  figure out a way to fold china into the  multilateral system she didn't think  that you could contain china's rise but  i think that she thought that there was  a role for germany in trying to manage  it now that line has come under  increasing criticism inside germany and  of course outside germany in recent  years and i think there's now a  consensus at least in some parts of the  german establishment that this  relationship with china is going to have  to be recalibrated on almost every  single front yeah do you agree with that  i think there is um  a lot of rethinking has to be done on  the part of the social democrats about  their  concept of engagement coming from the  old idea of us politik  from the cold war  changed through trade  we've never been closer  with with china than today uh in terms  of trade  but it hasn't uh amounted to a lot of  opening up of the chinese society quite  to the contrary i will stick my neck out  there and i think that this area is one  area where  it will take a while but german german  policy will change really quite  substantially towards china in the next  few years in large part because of what  is happening in china and how germany is  realizing what is happening in china i  think it's going to become a much more  pressing concern for the next chancellor  not because they will want it to be but  because events will force it on them i  think this will be the biggest foreign  policy test for the next german  government  hi that was part of the digital event  produced exclusively for subscribers to  the economist if you'd like to watch the  full conversation please click the link  you'll just have to register with the  economist if you haven't already or you  can subscribe to enjoy all of our  journalism thanks for watching

